cputlb: physical-address summary for tlb_flush_phys_ranges

0016 made a memory-topology commit drop only the TLB entries translating
into the changed physical ranges instead of flushing everything, but it
finds them by walking every entry of every mmu_idx.  0040 then grew the
table from 256 to 16384 entries, so that walk got 64x more expensive:

  measured, one S75v40lg1 boot: 16,019 commits walked 280,759,680 entries
  to invalidate 22,214 - 12,600 entries touched per entry dropped, and
  512 KB of table streamed through the cache on every romd flip.

Keep a per-mmu_idx summary of which 32 MB physical blocks the entries
translate into: one 64-bit mask per 64-entry group, plus the OR over the
groups and the victim table.  A commit ANDs the requested blocks against
it and skips whole tables and whole groups.  The masks are conservative
(bits are added on fill and on victim promotion, never removed on
eviction) and a commit that walks a group rewrites that group's mask
exactly, so staleness self-heals instead of accumulating.

The block size has to divide the ranges a commit reports - the flash
banks here are 32 MB and a first version with 64 MB blocks lumped the two
banks together, leaving 104 of 256 groups unskippable (280M -> 107M only).
At 32 MB: entries walked 280,759,680 -> 1,179,712 (237x), groups walked
1.16 per commit, entries dropped 22,007 -> 22,007 (identical: the filter
provably changes nothing but the search).

Boot effect on its own: FLAT (t1.3G 29.7 vs 29.8 s, both orders) - the
walk is a well-predicted streaming scan and costs ~0.3 ns/entry.  Kept as
part of the 0043-0045 stack (window -4 %, t0.5G -2 %, n=4 interleaved)
and because it makes the commit cost independent of TLB size, which 0040
and the flush-time resize policy can still raise.  Tables larger than
TLB_PHYS_GROUPS << TLB_PHYS_GROUP_BITS fall back to the full walk.

